WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.375
A SELECT query becomes much easier to understand
when you read it

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.888
like a sentence Start with SELECT This answers the
question what do

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.338
I want to see in the result You can list one column

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:18.900
several columns or expressions that calculate new
values Next is FROM This

00:00:18.925 --> 00:00:23.375
answers where does the data come from In our early
examples FROM

00:00:23.388 --> 00:00:27.712
points to one table Later when you learn joins a
query can

00:00:27.738 --> 00:00:33.425
combine data from several related tables WHERE is
optional It answers

00:00:33.612 --> 00:00:37.562
which rows should stay If we want only active
patients or only

00:00:37.575 --> 00:00:42.438
patients above a certain age the WHERE clause
defines those conditions ORDER

00:00:42.462 --> 00:00:46.888
BY controls how the final rows are arranged For
example we can

00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:50.938
sort age from highest to lowest LIMIT can then keep
only a

00:00:50.962 --> 00:00:55.388
certain number of rows So a useful reading pattern
is SELECT these

00:00:55.412 --> 00:00:58.888
columns FROM this table WHERE these conditions are
true ORDER the rows

00:00:58.912 --> 00:01:02.800
this way and LIMIT the result if needed A good
default is

00:01:02.825 --> 00:01:05.988
to list the columns you actually need instead of
always using SELECT

00:01:06.012 --> 00:01:11.875
star Explicit columns make the result clearer and
reduce unnecessary data retrieval

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.625
You will practice that immediately in the next few
slides
